  <data key="d5">1778752905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency">
  <data key="d0">National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency is a source of hazard information for geospatial products.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unmanned Aircraft System (UAS)">
  <data key="d0">Unmanned Aircraft System (UAS)</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Unmanned Aircraft Systems are aircraft systems that must be integrated into the overall air defense plan.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Remotely-piloted Aircraft (RPA)">
  <data key="d0">Remotely-piloted Aircraft (RPA)</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Remotely-piloted Aircraft are Groups 4 and 5 UASs operated by personnel trained to similar standards as manned aircraft pilots.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="UAS Categorization Chart">
  <data key="d0">UAS Categorization Chart</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The UAS Categorization Chart classifies unmanned aircraft into five groups based on weight, altitude, and speed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Air Defense Plan">
  <data key="d0">Air Defense Plan</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Air Defense Plan is an overall strategy into which new capabilities countering UAS systems must be integrated.&lt;SEP&gt;Air Defense Plan must integrate new capabilities to counter low, slow, and smaller UASs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Route Planning">
  <data key="d0">Route Planning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Route Planning is a process where planners must consider UAS lost-link contingency procedures.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lost-link Contingency Procedures">
  <data key="d0">Lost-link Contingency Procedures</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Lost-link Contingency Procedures are actions taken by a UAS when communication is lost, requiring announcement over tactical chat.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Operational Planning Products">
  <data key="d0">Operational Planning Products</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Operational Planning Products include maps and graphics that must be discernible from ground and air perspectives.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geospatial Products">
  <data key="d0">Geospatial Products</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Geospatial Products range from paper tourist maps to digital mapping databases and require continuous updates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common Operations Graphics">
  <data key="d0">Common Operations Graphics</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Common Operations Graphics depict objectives and phase lines to illustrate the ground force commander's scheme of maneuver.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gridded Reference Graphic (GRG)">
  <data key="d0">Gridded Reference Graphic (GRG)</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Gridded Reference Graphic is produced by overlaying a grid reference system on urban areas to identify buildings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752905</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Military Grid Reference System (MGRS)">
  <data key="d0">Military Grid Reference System (MGRS)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Military Grid Reference System is a technique used by joint planners to efficiently produce the required GRG.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="World Geodetic System 1984">
  <data key="d0">World Geodetic System 1984</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">World Geodetic System 1984 is the current joint standard reference system for preparing maps or charts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tokyo Special">
  <data key="d0">Tokyo Special</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Tokyo Special is a datum reference system that differs from World Geodetic System 1984 and can cause position errors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Airspace Control Elements">
  <data key="d0">Airspace Control Elements</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Airspace Control Elements include systems like AWACS, TAOC, and CRC used to relay lost-link situations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pilot">
  <data key="d0">Pilot</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Pilot is trained to similar standards as manned aircraft pilots and announces lost-link conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Operator">
  <data key="d0">Operator</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Operator controls the UAS and should announce a lost-link condition over tactical chat.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Planner">
  <data key="d0">Planner</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Planner considers UAS lost-link contingency procedures and ensures operational planning products are discernible.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intelligence Personnel">
  <data key="d0">Intelligence Personnel</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Intelligence Personnel may have the only source of hazard information required for aviation maps.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Operations Staff">
  <data key="d0">Operations Staff</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Operations Staff shares responsibility for maintaining accurate and current geospatial products.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Wasp III">
  <data key="d0">Wasp III</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Wasp III is a Group 1 Unmanned Aircraft System listed in the UAS Categorization Chart.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RQ-11B">
  <data key="d0">RQ-11B</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">RQ-11B is a Group 1 Unmanned Aircraft System listed in the UAS Categorization Chart.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MQ-1 Predator">
  <data key="d0">MQ-1 Predator</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">MQ-1 Predator is a Group 4 Unmanned Aircraft System listed in the UAS Categorization Chart.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MQ-9 Reaper">
  <data key="d0">MQ-9 Reaper</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">MQ-9 Reaper is a Group 5 Unmanned Aircraft System listed in the UAS Categorization Chart.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RQ-4 Global Hawk">
  <data key="d0">RQ-4 Global Hawk</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">RQ-4 Global Hawk is a Group 5 Unmanned Aircraft System listed in the UAS Categorization Chart.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Group 1">
  <data key="d0">Group 1</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Group 1 is a UAS category defined by maximum gross takeoff weight of 0-20 pounds and altitude below 1,200 feet AGL.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Group 2">
  <data key="d0">Group 2</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Group 2 is a UAS category defined by maximum gross takeoff weight of 21-55 pounds and altitude below 3,500 feet AGL.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Group 3">
  <data key="d0">Group 3</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Group 3 is a UAS category defined by maximum gross takeoff weight below 1,320 pounds and altitude below 18,000 feet MSL.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Group 4">
  <data key="d0">Group 4</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Group 4 is a UAS category defined by maximum gross takeoff weight below 1,320 pounds and any airspeed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752906</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Group 5">
  <data key="d0">Group 5</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Group 5 is a UAS category defined by maximum gross takeoff weight above 1,320 pounds and altitude above 18,000 feet MSL.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AWACS">
  <data key="d0">AWACS</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">AWACS is an airspace control element used to relay lost-link situations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TAOC">
  <data key="d0">TAOC</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">TAOC is an airspace control element used to relay lost-link situations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CRC">
  <data key="d0">CRC</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">CRC is an airspace control element used to relay lost-link situations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Notices To Airmen">
  <data key="d0">Notices To Airmen</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Notices To Airmen are known flight hazards published by the airspace control element.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f85140b13c2f4467aec323c7ad488b32</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cyberspace Domain">
  <data key="d0">Cyberspace Domain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Cyberspace Domain is an increasingly congested and contested environment where commanders must assure data access.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Electromagnetic Environment">
  <data key="d0">Electromagnetic Environment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Electromagnetic Environment is a contested domain where spectrum saturation occurs in urban areas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Airspace">
  <data key="d0">Airspace</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Airspace is the three-dimensional region where aviation assets operate and must be controlled.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Frequency Management">
  <data key="d0">Frequency Management</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Frequency Management is a planning method essential for voice and data spectrum users in urban environments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Airspace Control">
  <data key="d0">Airspace Control</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Airspace Control is a planning and execution method required to synchronize aviation assets and ground fires.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cyberspace Electromagnetic Activities">
  <data key="d0">Cyberspace Electromagnetic Activities</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Cyberspace Electromagnetic Activities are capabilities synchronized by commanders to support unified land operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unified Land Operations">
  <data key="d0">Unified Land Operations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Unified Land Operations are supported by planned and integrated cyberspace and electromagnetic warfare operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MEDEVAC">
  <data key="d0">MEDEVAC</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">MEDEVAC is an air medical evacuation event that may occur simultaneously with close air support in the same airspace.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unmanned Aircraft System">
  <data key="d0">Unmanned Aircraft System</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Unmanned Aircraft System is a general category of aircraft that includes SUAS and RPAs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RPA">
  <data key="d0">RPA</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">RPA are Remotely-Piloted Aircraft referred to by the Air Force as groups 4 and 5 UASs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ground Control Stations">
  <data key="d0">Ground Control Stations</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Ground Control Stations are equipment used to operate SUAS, often with limited waypoint programming.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Civilian Aircraft">
  <data key="d0">Civilian Aircraft</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Civilian Aircraft may be present in airspace following natural disasters without military communication.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752907</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Government Aircraft">
  <data key="d0">Government Aircraft</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Government Aircraft may be present in airspace following natural disasters without military communication.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752908</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radio Frequency">
  <data key="d0">Radio Frequency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Radio Frequency availability can be reduced in urban areas due to public safety and commercial usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752908</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aviation Assets">
  <data key="d0">Aviation Assets</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Aviation Assets are aircraft resources that must be synchronized with ground fires into the ground scheme of maneuver.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752908</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ground Maneuver Elements">
  <data key="d0">Ground Maneuver Elements</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Ground Maneuver Elements are military units with which aircrews should establish communications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752908</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Controlling Agencies">
  <data key="d0">Controlling Agencies</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Controlling Agencies are entities with which aircrews should establish communications before crossing airspace boundaries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752908</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Table 2">
  <data key="d0">Table 2</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 2 contains information about UAS categories referenced in the document.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b3a8a845280b83251233809b74dd7287</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN34839-ATP_3-06.1-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778752908</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Regional Frequency Manager">
