  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abu Sayyaf">
  <data key="d0">Abu Sayyaf</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Abu Sayyaf is an Al-Qaeda-linked terrorist group primarily combated by JSOTF-P.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759218</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jemaah Islamiyah">
  <data key="d0">Jemaah Islamiyah</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Jemaah Islamiyah is an Al-Qaeda-linked terrorist group primarily combated by JSOTF-P.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759218</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Government of the Philippines">
  <data key="d0">Government of the Philippines</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Government of the Philippines signed the Comprehensive Agreement on Bangsamoro in 2014.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759218</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Islamic State-Philippines">
  <data key="d0">Islamic State-Philippines</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Islamic State-Philippines (ISIS-P) is a force that Army special operations forces continue to operationally destabilize.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759218</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Indo-Pacific Command">
  <data key="d0">Indo-Pacific Command</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Indo-Pacific Command utilizes the partnership and presence in the Philippines as contributions to its campaign plan.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759218</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Secretary of Defense">
  <data key="d0">Secretary of Defense</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Secretary of Defense has the authority to approve directed special operations activities.&lt;SEP&gt;The Secretary of Defense requires reporting on all activities conducted under military information support operations authorities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83b849ddc9c6eed87498b4d0cd5715fc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759218</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Congress">
  <data key="d0">Congress</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Congress requires reporting on all activities conducted under military information support operations authorities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759218</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Psychological Operations Units">
  <data key="d0">Psychological Operations Units</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Psychological Operations Units are the lead element for military information support operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759218</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mindanao">
  <data key="d0">Mindanao</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Mindanao is the region where the Comprehensive Agreement on Bangsamoro recognized and included resistance group members in the government.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Southeast Asia">
  <data key="d0">Southeast Asia</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Southeast Asia is the region where the United States maintains a forward presence through partnerships.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Indo-Pacific">
  <data key="d0">Indo-Pacific</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">The Indo-Pacific is the region covered by the Indo-Pacific Command campaign plan.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U.S. Global War on Terror">
  <data key="d0">U.S. Global War on Terror</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The U.S. Global War on Terror was the broader initiative under which JSOTF-P was established in 2002.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Comprehensive Agreement on Bangsamoro">
  <data key="d0">Comprehensive Agreement on Bangsamoro</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Comprehensive Agreement on Bangsamoro was signed in 2014 to recognize resistance group members and defuse destabilization desires.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Balikatan">
  <data key="d0">Balikatan</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Balikatan is an exercise through which Army special operations forces continue to support the Philippines.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="World War II">
  <data key="d0">World War II</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">World War II is cited as an example where support to resistances was used as unconventional warfare.&lt;SEP&gt;World War II is a historical conflict where support to resistances was used to create security dilemmas in enemy rear areas.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe9b99ab479688bf15c5eb6b71313a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Psychological Operations">
  <data key="d0">Psychological Operations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Psychological Operations are conducted by units to create conditions for commanders to employ influence activities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Special Reconnaissance">
  <data key="d0">Special Reconnaissance</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Special reconnaissance is an assigned special operations activity conducted by special operations forces.&lt;SEP&gt;Special Reconnaissance involves the collection of strategic or operational information to inform planning and decision making.&lt;SEP&gt;Special Reconnaissance is conducted as part of the range of activities to counter weapons of mass destruction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f143cf228a70fcc3b24d3886031aa2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83b849ddc9c6eed87498b4d0cd5715fc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unconventional Warfare">
  <data key="d0">Unconventional Warfare</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Unconventional warfare is an assigned special operations activity conducted by special operations forces.&lt;SEP&gt;Unconventional Warfare consists of activities conducted to enable a resistance movement or insurgency to coerce or overthrow a government.&lt;SEP&gt;Unconventional warfare is a method employed to coerce, disrupt, or overthrow a government or occupying power using underground, auxiliary, and guerrilla forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe9b99ab479688bf15c5eb6b71313a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83b849ddc9c6eed87498b4d0cd5715fc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DODI O-3607.02">
  <data key="d0">DODI O-3607.02</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">DODI O-3607.02 is a document referenced for requirements to obtain authority for military information support operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CJCSI 3110.05G">
  <data key="d0">CJCSI 3110.05G</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">CJCSI 3110.05G is a document referenced for requirements to obtain authority for military information support operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JP 3-53">
  <data key="d0">JP 3-53</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">JP 3-53 is a document referenced for requirements to obtain authority for military information support operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resistance Movement">
  <data key="d0">Resistance Movement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Resistance Movement is enabled by unconventional warfare to coerce, disrupt, or overthrow a government.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759219</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Civil Affairs">
  <data key="d0">Civil Affairs</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Civil Affairs units lived and worked with partners in the Philippines as part of JSOTF-P special operations activities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="2015">
  <data key="d0">2015</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The year the Joint Special Operations Task Force-Philippines officially deactivated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Army Forces">
  <data key="d0">Army Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Army forces enable the forces of a host nation to maintain internal stability and counter threats.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3f9296b5d072caec55997f55d6e957ea</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="National And Combatant Command Objectives">
  <data key="d0">National And Combatant Command Objectives</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">National And Combatant Command Objectives are broader goals that policies, plans, and operations must align with to avoid undermining legitimacy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conventional Forces">
  <data key="d0">Conventional Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Conventional Forces are integrated with Army special operations forces to enhance operational effectiveness and achieve synergistic effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Conventional Forces are military units that utilize sheer firepower and mass, often providing support to special operations forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b8ed0cc5c4a8e919c8f12065962561e</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U.S. Army">
  <data key="d0">U.S. Army</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The U.S. Army conducts counter threat finance activities as a critical component of broader efforts to counter security threats.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Army organizes its medical care system into four roles to provide tiered medical support.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f143cf228a70fcc3b24d3886031aa2</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Role 1 Medical Treatment">
  <data key="d0">Role 1 Medical Treatment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Role 1 Medical Treatment is unit-level care organic to most Army special operations forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Role 2 Level Care">
  <data key="d0">Role 2 Level Care</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Role 2 Level Care involves advanced trauma management and emergency surgery, provided on a limited basis by specific units.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Role 3 Capability">
  <data key="d0">Role 3 Capability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Role 3 Capability includes emergency and specialty surgery and intensive care, which Army special operations forces lack organically.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Role 4">
  <data key="d0">Role 4</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Role 4 provides a full range of preventive, acute, restorative, and rehabilitative care within the medical system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Special Forces Medical Sergeants">
  <data key="d0">Special Forces Medical Sergeants</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Special Forces Medical Sergeants are personnel who provide Role 1 medical treatment within Army special operations forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Civil Affairs Medical Sergeants">
  <data key="d0">Civil Affairs Medical Sergeants</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Civil Affairs Medical Sergeants are personnel who provide Role 1 medical treatment within Army special operations forces.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Special Forces Groups">
  <data key="d0">Special Forces Groups</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Special Forces Groups are Army special operations forces elements capable of providing Role 2 level care on a limited basis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Far East Command">
  <data key="d0">Far East Command</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Far East Command created an ad-hoc Ranger company during the Korean War to interdict enemy supply lines.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Eighth Army Ranger Company">
  <data key="d0">Eighth Army Ranger Company</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Eighth Army Ranger Company was an ad-hoc unit created to interdict supply lines and later attached to the 25th Infantry Division.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eighth Army Ranger Company engaged in combat at Hill 205 and was eventually disbanded due to being rendered combat ineffective.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db557cf9378c4bf4f84c616fc05e03ba</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Eighth Army">
  <data key="d0">Eighth Army</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Eighth Army served as the higher command under which the ad-hoc Ranger company was initially created.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="25th Infantry Division">
  <data key="d0">25th Infantry Division</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The 25th Infantry Division had the Eighth Army Ranger Company attached to it during the drive north in the Korean War.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759220</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="North Korean Troops">
  <data key="d0">North Korean Troops</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">North Korean Troops launched a surprise invasion of South Korea in June 1950.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759221</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="South Korea">
  <data key="d0">South Korea</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">South Korea was invaded by North Korean troops, leading to the defense of the Pusan perimeter.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5c39d7c4f2e0018da66a43c03ab90bc</data>
