  <data key="d0">Seven Phases Of Unconventional Warfare</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The seven phases of unconventional warfare are a conceptual template including Preparation, Initial Contact, Infiltration, Organization, Buildup, Employment, and Transition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fe9b99ab479688bf15c5eb6b71313a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759225</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="U.S. Military Forces">
  <data key="d0">U.S. Military Forces</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">U.S. military forces must coordinate activities with mission partners in unconventional warfare scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fe9b99ab479688bf15c5eb6b71313a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759225</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Counterproliferation">
  <data key="d0">Counterproliferation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Counterproliferation refers to actions taken to reduce the risks posed by extant weapons of mass destruction to the United States, allies, and partners.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fe9b99ab479688bf15c5eb6b71313a3b</data>
  <data key="d4">D:\Academic resources\ARN44116-FM_3-05-000-WEB-1.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1778759225</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
